","Ġthe","Ġright","Ġpanel","Ġby","Ġchance","."],"decoded_tokens":["A"," repair"," to"," a"," bridge"," has"," brought"," an"," unexpected"," archive"," to"," light","\n\n","Workers"," inspect","ing"," a"," foot","bridge"," in"," the"," town"," of"," Low"," Ash"," found"," a"," sealed"," metal"," box"," containing"," maintenance"," notes"," and"," photographs"," of"," earlier"," repairs",".\n\n","The"," papers"," were"," placed"," there"," by"," a"," previous"," works"," team",","," apparently"," to"," help"," whoever"," came"," next","."," Some"," drawings"," record"," changes"," that"," do"," not"," appear"," in"," the"," council","'s"," surviving"," plans",".\n\n","Engine","ers"," are"," checking"," the"," notes"," against"," the"," structure"," rather"," than"," assuming"," they"," are"," accurate","."," A"," penc","illed"," annotation",","," for"," example",","," describes"," a"," proposed"," alteration"," without"," confirming"," that"," it"," was"," built",".\n\n","The"," discovery"," will"," not"," delay"," the"," current"," safety"," work","."," Conserv","ators"," have"," photographed"," the"," contents",","," and"," the"," originals"," will"," be"," stored"," away"," from"," the"," damp"," bridge",".\n\n","When"," the"," repair"," is"," finished",","," the"," council"," plans"," to"," put"," a"," new"," set"," of"," records"," in"," its"," ordinary"," archive",","," with"," a"," reference"," attached"," to"," the"," bridge","."," Officials"," say"," finding"," useful"," documents"," should"," not"," depend"," on"," opening"," the"," right"," panel"," by"," chance","."],"ids_available":true,"boundaries_approximate":false,"piece_format":"native_vocabulary_piece","decoded_text":"A repair to a bridge has brought an unexpected archive to light\n\nWorkers inspecting a footbridge in the town of Low Ash found a sealed metal box containing maintenance notes and photographs of earlier repairs.\n\nThe papers were placed there by a previous works team, apparently to help whoever came next. Some drawings record changes that do not appear in the council's surviving plans.\n\nEngineers are checking the notes against the structure rather than assuming they are accurate. A pencilled annotation, for example, describes a proposed alteration without confirming that it was built.\n\nThe discovery will not delay the current safety work. Conservators have photographed the contents, and the originals will be stored away from the damp bridge.\n\nWhen the repair is finished, the council plans to put a new set of records in its ordinary archive, with a reference attached to the bridge. Officials say finding useful documents should not depend on opening the right panel by chance.","measurement_context":null,"character_measurement":null,"roundtrip_exact":true},{"id":"news-en-0006","collection":"dataset/news/en.jsonl","text":"A museum removes the word “unknown” from some labels — and adds questions\n\nAt the Northbank Museum, a display of household objects is being rewritten. Labels that once said only “maker unknown” now explain what researchers have checked and what evidence is missing.\n\nOne wooden box carries a workshop mark, but no surviving record links it to a named craftsperson. Rather than guess, the museum shows a photograph of the mark and invites comparisons with other collections.\n\nCurators say visitors often interpreted the old wording as a final conclusion. The new labels make clear that uncertainty can be the starting point of an investigation.\n\nNot every suggestion will alter the catalogue. Submissions will be assessed, and proposed changes will remain separate from confirmed information.\n\nThe museum has also retained copies of previous labels. Staff say that recording how an interpretation changes is part of looking after an object, even when the earlier explanation now seems incomplete.","token_ids":[32,23904,28063,279,3409,1036,16074,854,504,1045,9198,1959,323,11362,4755,271,1655,279,4787,17014,16314,11,264,3037,315,1372